              "modelAnswer": "a) Branded vs Generic drug (8 marks):\n• Branded (innovator) drug: marketed under a proprietary trade name by the company that developed it; usually more expensive; has patent protection during the exclusivity period.\n• Generic drug: marketed under the international non-proprietary name (INN) or a non-proprietary brand; identical in active ingredient, strength, dosage form, route of administration and intended use; must be bioequivalent (same rate and extent of absorption) to the innovator product; usually cheaper because no development/marketing cost recovery.\n• Both contain the same active pharmaceutical ingredient; the difference is in name, price and marketing, not in therapeutic effect if bioequivalent.\n• Explain to the patient: 'The tablets contain the same medicine; the generic has a different name and costs less, but acts in the same way.'\n\nb) Chemical name (2 marks):\nThe chemical name describes the drug's molecular structure according to chemical nomenclature rules (e.g., IUPAC name), e.g., N-acetyl-para-aminophenol for paracetamol. It is the scientific description of the molecule, too complex for everyday use.\n\nc) Five ideal properties of drugs (5 marks) — any five:\n1. Effectiveness — produces the desired therapeutic effect reliably.\n2. Safety — no harmful or toxic effects at therapeutic doses (high therapeutic index).\n3. Selectivity — acts only on the desired target/tissue with few off-target effects.\n4. Reversibility — effects are reversible (antagonists/agonists can be counteracted).\n5. Ease of administration — convenient route (oral preferred), acceptable to patients.\n6. Freedom from drug interactions — does not interact adversely with other drugs/foods.\n7. Low cost — affordable.\n8. Chemical stability — stable in storage and in the body.\n9. Simple generic name — easy to remember and prescribe."
