              "stem": "Describe PROPRANOLOL under the following headings:\nMode of action(2 Marks)\nAny seven Clinical applications(7 Marks)\nAny six side effects(3 Marks)\nany three drug-drug interactions(6 Marks)\nContraindications(2 Marks)",
              "modelAnswer": "PROPRANOLOL\n\nMode of action (2 marks): Nonselective competitive antagonist at β1- and β2-adrenoceptors — blocks catecholamine effects on the heart (β1: reduced rate, contractility, conduction) and on bronchial/vascular smooth muscle and metabolic tissues (β2). Membrane-stabilising effect only at high doses.\n\nSeven clinical applications (7 marks) — any seven:\n1. Hypertension — reduces cardiac output and renin release.\n2. Angina pectoris — reduces myocardial oxygen demand.\n3. Post-myocardial infarction — secondary prevention (reduces mortality/arrhythmias).\n4. Cardiac arrhythmias — supraventricular tachyarrhythmias, rate control in atrial fibrillation.\n5. Heart failure — compensated stable HFrEF (initiated cautiously, uptitrated).\n6. Migraine prophylaxis — reduces frequency of attacks.\n7. Essential tremor — symptomatic control.\n8. Thyrotoxicosis/hyperthyroidism — controls tachycardia, tremor, anxiety.\n9. Glaucoma (topical) — reduces aqueous humour production.\n10. Portal hypertension — prophylaxis of variceal bleeding (with oesophageal varices).\n11. Performance/situational anxiety. (Pheochromocytoma only after α-blockade.)\n\nSix side effects (3 marks): 1. Bradycardia and hypotension; 2. Bronchoconstriction (β2-blockade — avoid in asthma/COPD); 3. Fatigue, lethargy and reduced exercise tolerance; 4. Cold extremities / Raynaud's phenomenon; 5. CNS effects — depression, nightmares, insomnia; 6. Masking of hypoglycaemia (blunts tachycardia/tremor in insulin-treated diabetics); also impotence/erectile dysfunction, heart failure precipitation, AV block, gastrointestinal upset.\n\nThree drug–drug interactions (6 marks):\n1. Verapamil/diltiazem (calcium-channel blockers) — additive depression of SA/AV node and myocardial contractility (risk of bradycardia, heart block, hypotension).\n2. CYP2D6 inhibitors (cimetidine, fluoxetine, paroxetine, quinidine) — increase propranolol levels → enhanced effects/toxicity; enzyme inducers (rifampicin, phenobarbitone) reduce levels.\n3. NSAIDs — reduce the antihypertensive effect; insulin/oral hypoglycaemics — enhanced and masked hypoglycaemia; sympathomimetics may cause unopposed α-mediated hypertension.\n\nContraindications (2 marks): Asthma/COPD (bronchospasm); sinus bradycardia; 2nd/3rd-degree AV block; decompensated (acute) heart failure; cardiogenic shock; sick sinus syndrome; severe peripheral vascular disease/Raynaud's; (untreated phaeochromocytoma — unopposed α stimulation)."
