              "modelAnswer": "Stepwise management of anaphylaxis (bee stings — Y.S):\n1. Assess and secure the airway, breathing and circulation (ABCDE); call for help; remove the trigger (scrape out the bee stinger); stop any drug infusions.\n2. ADRENALINE (epinephrine) is first-line: IM 0.5 mg (0.5 mL of 1:1000) into the anterolateral thigh, repeat every 5 minutes as needed (IV infusion in refractory shock under monitoring).\n3. Give high-flow oxygen; position the patient supine with legs elevated (or sitting up if breathing difficulty is severe).\n4. Rapid IV crystalloid fluid bolus (e.g., 20 mL/kg) for hypotension/shock.\n5. Adjunctive drugs: chlorpheniramine (H1 antagonist) IV/IM + ranitidine (H2 antagonist) IV; hydrocortisone IV to help prevent the late (biphasic) reaction; nebulised salbutamol for bronchospasm.\n6. Continuous monitoring; observe for 6–24 hours (risk of biphasic anaphylaxis); arrange adrenaline auto-injector prescription and allergy referral before discharge."
