              "stem": "GABA is a target for different drugs. Describe with an example in each case how drugs can target GABA. (4 marks)",
              "modelAnswer": "Drugs can target the GABA system in the following ways:\n1. Direct GABA-A receptor agonists — e.g., MUSCIMOL (activates the GABA recognition site itself).\n2. Positive allosteric modulation of GABA-A (enhance GABA action) — e.g., BENZODIAZEPINES (diazepam: increases frequency of Cl- channel opening) and BARBITURATES (phenobarbitone: increases duration of Cl- channel opening), also propofol and ethanol.\n3. GABA-A receptor/channel antagonists — e.g., BICUCULLINE (competitive antagonist at the GABA recognition site), PICROTOXIN (blocks the Cl- channel).\n4. GABA-B receptor agonism — e.g., BACLOFEN (metabotropic receptor; used in spasticity).\n5. Inhibition of GABA metabolism — e.g., VIGABATRIN (irreversible inhibitor of GABA transaminase → ↑GABA) and sodium valproate (inhibits GABA-T at high concentrations).\n6. Inhibition of GABA reuptake (GAT-1) — e.g., TIAGABINE (↑synaptic GABA).\n7. Benzodiazepine antagonism — FLUMAZENIL (competitive antagonist at the BZ site; used to reverse benzodiazepine effects)."
