              "stem": "Mention the different types of angina and recommend a drug for management of each type of angina. (6 marks)",
              "modelAnswer": "Types of angina and recommended drugs:\n1. CLASSIC (stable/exertional) ANGINA — due to fixed atherosclerotic obstruction; pain on exertion, relieved by rest.\n   • Acute relief: sublingual nitroglycerin (short-acting nitrate).\n   • Prophylaxis: β-blockers (metoprolol, atenolol), long-acting nitrates (isosorbide dinitrate/mononitrate), calcium channel blockers (amlodipine, diltiazem); plus aspirin and a statin for secondary prevention.\n2. PRINZMETAL (variant/vasospastic) ANGINA — due to coronary artery spasm, often at rest.\n   • Drug of choice: calcium channel blockers (nifedipine, verapamil, diltiazem); nitrates (sublingual/IV) for acute attacks; AVOID β-blockers (may worsen spasm by leaving α-mediated vasoconstriction unopposed).\n3. UNSTABLE ANGINA (crescendo/rest angina — acute coronary syndrome without ST elevation) — due to plaque rupture/thrombosis.\n   • Management: aspirin + clopidogrel (dual antiplatelet), heparin/LMWH (anticoagulation), nitrates, β-blockers; consider coronary angiography/PCI.\n(All types: sublingual GTN for acute episodes; treat underlying risk factors.)"
