              "stem": "Describe the factors that contribute to the decline in the use of methylxanthines as first line agents in the management of asthma(5marks)",
              "modelAnswer": "Factors contributing to the decline of methylxanthines as first-line asthma agents (5 marks):\n1. NARROW THERAPEUTIC INDEX — the therapeutic range (5–15 mg/L) is narrow; above 20 mg/L serious toxicity occurs (nausea, tremors, tachycardia, cardiac arrhythmias, seizures), requiring therapeutic drug monitoring.\n2. LARGE VARIATION IN CLEARANCE — wide interindividual and intraindividual variability in hepatic metabolism (CYP1A2): influenced by age, smoking, liver disease, heart failure, and numerous drug interactions (cimetidine, macrolides, fluoroquinolones increase levels; phenytoin, rifampicin, smoking decrease them).\n3. AVAILABILITY OF SAFER, MORE EFFECTIVE DRUGS — inhaled short-acting β2-agonists (rapid symptom relief) and inhaled corticosteroids (the cornerstone anti-inflammatory controller) have replaced methylxanthines as first-line agents.\n4. GUIDELINE PLACEMENT — international asthma guidelines (GINA) position inhaled corticosteroids as first-line controllers, with theophylline only as an add-on third-line option in severe disease.\n5. POOR ROUTE OF ADMINISTRATION — ineffective by inhalation (only oral/IV), and oral dosing is associated with systemic adverse effects (GI upset, CNS stimulation, cardiac effects).\n6. SIDE-EFFECT PROFILE — GI (nausea, vomiting), CNS (insomnia, anxiety, seizures) and cardiac (palpitations, arrhythmias) effects limit patient acceptability and dose titration."
