              "stem": "Match each gastrointestinal drug with the appropriate description (7 Marks)\nDrugs: Erythromycin, omeprazole, Magnesium hydroxide, Misoprostol, Sucralfate, metoclopramide, Docusate, Aprepitant, Aluminium hydroxide, Ranitidine, Mesalamine, Bisacodyl\nDescriptions:\n1. A drug that can increase intestinal peristalsis by activating motilin receptors\n2. An antiulcer agent that can inhibit 24-hour gastric acid secretion up to 95%\n3. A salicylate derivative used in inflammatory bowel diseases\n4. An antiemetic drug used primarily for chemotherapy-induced vomiting\n5. An antacid that also has laxative properties\n6. A prostaglandin E1 analogue that can have a cytoprotective effect on gastric mucosa\n7. An antiulcer agent that binds to necrotic peptic ulcer tissue, thus acting as a barrier to gastric juice (This is a small molecule that polymerizes in stomach acid and coats the ulcer bed, resulting in accelerated healing and reduction of symptoms.)",
              "modelAnswer": "1 – Erythromycin: activates motilin receptors on GI smooth muscle/enteric neurons → increases intestinal peristalsis (prokinetic).\n2 – Omeprazole: proton pump inhibitor — irreversibly inhibits H+/K+-ATPase in parietal cells → inhibits 24-hour gastric acid secretion up to 95%.\n3 – Mesalamine: a 5-aminosalicylic acid (salicylate) derivative used topically in inflammatory bowel disease.\n4 – Aprepitant: neurokinin-1 (NK1) receptor antagonist — antiemetic used primarily for chemotherapy-induced vomiting.\n5 – Magnesium hydroxide: antacid that also has osmotic laxative properties.\n6 – Misoprostol: prostaglandin E1 analogue with cytoprotective effects on the gastric mucosa (stimulates mucus/bicarbonate secretion, mucosal blood flow).\n7 – Sucralfate: binds to necrotic peptic ulcer tissue and polymerizes in gastric acid to form a protective barrier over the ulcer bed, accelerating healing."
