              "modelAnswer": "a) 'BROAD-SPECTRUM' ANTIBIOTICS (1 mark): Antibiotics active against a wide range of organisms — both gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria (and often anaerobes), e.g., third-generation cephalosporins, clindamycin, fluoroquinolones — as opposed to narrow-spectrum agents targeting specific organisms.\nb) TWO ANTIBIOTICS (DIFFERENT CLASSES) ASSOCIATED WITH PSEUDOMEMBRANOUS COLITIS (2 marks): Clindamycin (lincosamide) — the classic culprit; also ampicillin/amoxicillin (aminopenicillins), cephalosporins (beta-lactams), and fluoroquinolones (e.g., ciprofloxacin).\nc) TWO ANTIBIOTICS TO MANAGE CDI WITH ROUTES (2 marks): Oral VANCOMYCIN (preferred — oral route, not absorbed, acts intraluminally) and oral FIDAXOMICIN; alternatively oral/IV METRONIDAZOLE (for mild disease if vancomycin unavailable)."
