              "modelAnswer": "a) THREE REASONABLE EMPIRIC ANTIBIOTICS FOR A COMPLICATED UTI (3 marks):\n1. Oral ciprofloxacin (or levofloxacin) — fluoroquinolone, good renal parenchymal penetration.\n2. Oral amoxicillin-clavulanate (co-amoxiclav).\n3. Oral cefpodoxime/cefixime (third-generation oral cephalosporin) — or nitrofurantoin for lower-tract infection (though less suitable here given fever/flank pain).\n(Note: TMP-SMX is an option only if local resistance < 20%; her three recent TMP-SMX-treated infections make resistance more likely.)\nb) POTENTIAL DRUG INTERACTIONS (2 marks):\n1. Fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin/levofloxacin) CHELATE divalent cations — the patient's daily CALCIUM supplement reduces fluoroquinolone absorption (take fluoroquinolone 2 hours before or 6 hours after calcium).\n2. TMP-SMX can potentiate warfarin (CYP2C9 inhibition) and raise potassium (trimethoprim is amiloride-like); it also has weaker activity if she has already failed it.\n3. Amoxicillin-clavulanate may reduce the efficacy of oral contraceptives (minor)."
