              "modelAnswer": "PENICILLINS (each sub-topic addressed):\n1) MECHANISM OF ACTION OF BENZYLPENICILLIN (3 marks): Benzylpenicillin inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis: it binds penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs/transpeptidases), blocking the transpeptidation (cross-linking) of peptidoglycan strands in the growing cell wall; it also inactivates the cell-wall autolysin inhibitor, activating autolysins that destroy the wall. Result: cell lysis — BACTERICIDAL, active only against actively dividing organisms.\n2) SPECTRUM OF AMOXICILLIN (3 marks): Acid-stable, orally absorbed aminopenicillin with benzylpenicillin's gram-positive activity PLUS extended gram-negative coverage — H. influenzae, E. coli, Salmonella, Shigella, Proteus mirabilis, Listeria; active against H. pylori (in combinations) and enterococci. NOT active against beta-lactamase-producing organisms (hence combined with clavulanate → co-amoxiclav).\n3) SIX PHARMACOKINETIC PROPERTIES OF BENZYLPENICILLIN (3 marks): (1) Acid-labile — destroyed by gastric acid, so it must be given parenterally (IM/IV); (2) short plasma half-life (~30 min) — requires frequent dosing (or procaine/benzathine salts for depot); (3) protein binding ~60% (albumin); (4) wide distribution but POOR CNS penetration unless meninges are inflamed; (5) excreted largely unchanged by the kidney via glomerular filtration AND active tubular secretion; (6) probenecid blocks its tubular secretion, raising and prolonging plasma levels; (7) poorly lipid-soluble, does not enter mammalian cells.\n4) THREE CLINICAL USES OF BENZYLPENICILLIN (3 marks): (1) Pneumococcal pneumonia and pneumococcal meningitis; (2) streptococcal infections — pharyngitis (S. pyogenes), rheumatic fever prophylaxis, cellulitis; (3) meningococcal infection/meningitis; also syphilis (Treponema pallidum), gas gangrene/anaerobic infections (Clostridia), actinomycosis.\n5) ADVERSE EFFECTS OF BENZYLPENICILLIN (3 marks): (1) HYPERSENSITIVITY — most important: maculopapular rash, urticaria, angioedema, anaphylaxis, drug fever (cross-reactivity with cephalosporins); (2) NEUROTOXICITY — high doses (or renal failure) → convulsions, especially intrathecal; (3) Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction when treating syphilis (fever, chills, exacerbation of lesions); also pain/inflammation at IM injection sites, Na+ overload (high-dose Na penicillin), haemolytic anaemia and neutropenia (rare)."
