              "stem": "Mr. SD is a 25-year-old man with a history of drug use, for which he is prescribed daily supervised methadone. He was released from prison 2 years ago. He also has a history of chronic alcohol consumption and previous treatment for pulmonary tuberculosis with unclear history on completion of treatment. He has been referred to a chest physician with a cough productive of sputum and fever (2 months in duration). A sputum sample, obtained by his primary care doctor, is positive for acid, alcohol-fast bacilli, and a chest radiograph shows right upper lobe cavities. Gene Expert studies show that Mycobacterium TB is resistant to Rifampicin\nElucidate a probable effective five drug regimen that would be used for the treatment of multi-drug resistant TB in Mr. SD (5 Marks)\nFor each of the drug in (a) above, explain the mechanism of action (5 Marks)\n(b) For each drug suggested in (a) above identify at least one adverse effect associated with it (5 Marks)",
              "modelAnswer": "MDR-TB REGIMEN (rifampicin-resistant TB) — a probable effective FIVE-DRUG regimen:\na) FIVE-DRUG REGIMEN (5 marks): Since the isolate is rifampicin-resistant, rifampicin is replaced/omitted. A probable regimen: (1) Isoniazid, (2) Pyrazinamide, (3) Ethambutol, (4) a fluoroquinolone — Moxifloxacin (or levofloxacin), and (5) an injectable agent — Amikacin (or kanamycin/capreomycin). [Accept also: linezolid, bedaquiline, clofazimine, cycloserine, para-aminosalicylic acid per WHO group A/B drugs. Note: supervised daily therapy is important in this patient (methadone supervision can be leveraged for DOT); alcohol history requires liver monitoring.]\nb) MECHANISM OF ACTION OF EACH DRUG (5 marks):\n- Isoniazid: inhibits mycolic acid synthesis in the mycobacterial cell wall (activated by catalase-peroxidase KatG; inhibits InhA).\n- Pyrazinamide: converted to pyrazinoic acid in acidic (intracellular) environments; disrupts membrane transport/energy production — active against semi-dormant bacilli.\n- Ethambutol: inhibits arabinosyl transferase → blocks arabinogalactan synthesis in the mycobacterial cell wall.\n- Moxifloxacin/levofloxacin (fluoroquinolone): inhibits bacterial DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II) → prevents DNA supercoiling/replication.\n- Amikacin (aminoglycoside): binds the 30S ribosomal subunit → mRNA misreading/abnormal proteins → bactericidal.\nc) AT LEAST ONE ADVERSE EFFECT FOR EACH DRUG (5 marks):\n- Isoniazid: hepatotoxicity, peripheral neuropathy (pyridoxine deficiency — give vitamin B6), CNS toxicity.\n- Pyrazinamide: hepatotoxicity, hyperuricaemia/gout, GI upset.\n- Ethambutol: retrobulbar (optic) neuritis — red-green colour blindness, blurred vision (monitor visual acuity; dose in renal impairment).\n- Moxifloxacin: QT prolongation, GI disturbance, tendonitis/tendon rupture, dysglycaemia.\n- Amikacin: ototoxicity (hearing loss/vertigo) and nephrotoxicity; also neuromuscular blockade."
