              "stem": "A patient diagnosed with HER-2 positive breast cancer is given cyclophosphamide, methotrexate, 5-fluorouracil and trastuzumab.\nDescribe the mode of action of the above mentioned drugs?(8 marks)\nMESNA and Leucovorin were also given to this patient. Propose the reasons necessitating administration of MESNA and leucovorin.(3 marks)\nIf the patient was HER2 negative, which drug mentioned above would not have been prescribed and why?(2 marks)\nIf the patient was diagnosed with ER-positive breast cancer instead of HER-2 positive breast cancer,\nWhich other drug should have been administered to this patient?(1 mark)\nGive ONE reason for administering the drug in i) above.(1 mark)",
              "modelAnswer": "HER-2 POSITIVE BREAST CANCER — CMF + TRASTUZUMAB:\na) MODE OF ACTION (8 marks):\n- Cyclophosphamide: alkylating agent — activated in the liver to phosphoramide mustard → alkylates/cross-links DNA strands → blocks DNA replication → cell death (cell-cycle non-specific).\n- Methotrexate: antifolate antimetabolite — inhibits dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR) → depletes tetrahydrofolate → blocks thymidylate and purine synthesis → inhibits DNA synthesis (S-phase specific).\n- 5-Fluorouracil (5-FU): antimetabolite — converted to 5-FdUMP, which irreversibly inhibits thymidylate synthase → blocks thymidylate synthesis → inhibits DNA synthesis (S-phase specific).\n- Trastuzumab: humanized monoclonal antibody against the HER2/neu (ErbB2) receptor — binds the extracellular domain → blocks HER2 signaling, down-regulates the receptor, and triggers antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity (ADCC) against HER2-overexpressing tumor cells.\nb) REASONS FOR MESNA AND LEUCOVORIN (3 marks):\n- MESNA: inactivates acrolein, the urotoxic metabolite of cyclophosphamide, in the urinary tract → prevents hemorrhagic cystitis (given with high-dose cyclophosphamide).\n- LEUCOVORIN (folinic acid): 'rescue' therapy — provides reduced folate that bypasses the methotrexate-induced DHFR blockade, rescuing normal (and tumor) cells; also enhances 5-FU cytotoxicity by stabilizing the ternary complex of thymidylate synthase.\nc) IF HER2-NEGATIVE (2 marks): Trastuzumab would NOT be prescribed — there is no HER2 receptor over-expression/target for the antibody to bind, so it would have no therapeutic effect.\nd) IF ER-POSITIVE (1 + 1 marks):\ni) Add TAMOXIFEN (a selective estrogen receptor modulator) — or an aromatase inhibitor (e.g., anastrozole) in post-menopausal women.\nii) Reason: ER-positive breast cancer growth is driven by estrogen; tamoxifen blocks estrogen receptors on tumor cells → inhibits estrogen-stimulated proliferation → reduces recurrence and mortality."
