              "stem": "For fundoscopic examination, the ophthalmologist decided to use atropine eye drops.\nDescribe the mode of action of atropine in this scenario?(2 marks)\nMention any FOUR (4) drugs that can also be used for fundoscopic examination.(4 marks)\nOutline other SIX (6) clinical applications of atropine.(6 marks)\nList SIX (6) side effects of atropine(3 marks)",
              "modelAnswer": "ATROPINE FOR FUNDOSCOPIC EXAMINATION:\na) MODE OF ACTION (2 marks): Atropine is a competitive muscarinic (anticholinergic) receptor antagonist. In the eye it blocks M3 receptors on the iris sphincter pupillae → MYDRIASIS (pupil dilatation), and on the ciliary muscle → CYCLOPLEGIA (paralysis of accommodation) — allowing full examination of the fundus.\nb) FOUR OTHER DRUGS FOR FUNDOSCOPY (4 marks): Tropicamide; Cyclopentolate; Homatropine; Phenylephrine (alpha-1 adrenergic agonist — mydriatic only, no cycloplegia). (Any four.)\nc) SIX OTHER CLINICAL APPLICATIONS OF ATROPINE (6 marks):\n1. Pre-anaesthetic medication — reduces salivary/bronchial secretions (antisialagogue) and prevents vagal bradycardia.\n2. Treatment of bradyarrhythmias — sinus bradycardia and AV block (IV atropine).\n3. Antidote for organophosphate/anticholinesterase poisoning (with pralidoxime).\n4. Antidote for cholinergic excess/mushroom poisoning (muscarine).\n5. Antispasmodic in gastrointestinal disorders — peptic ulcer, irritable bowel (reduces motility and secretion).\n6. Bronchodilation in asthma/COPD (though inhaled ipratropium is preferred).\n7. Uveitis/iritis — mydriasis and cycloplegia prevent posterior synechiae formation.\n8. Urinary incontinence/overactive bladder — antispasmodic effect on the detrusor. (Any six.)\nd) SIX SIDE EFFECTS OF ATROPINE (3 marks): Dry mouth (xerostomia); blurred vision (cycloplegia); photophobia; tachycardia; urinary retention; constipation; dry/flushed skin with hyperthermia (anhidrosis); CNS effects — restlessness, confusion, hallucinations (central anticholinergic syndrome). (Any six.)"
