              "modelAnswer": "a) DRUG (1 mark): Propylthiouracil (PTU) — the antithyroid drug of choice in the FIRST trimester of pregnancy (methimazole is teratogenic — aplasia cutis, embryopathy).\nb) MODE OF ACTION (2 marks): PTU inhibits thyroid peroxidase → blocks iodination (organification) of tyrosine residues and coupling of iodotyrosines → inhibits synthesis of new T3/T4. In addition, PTU (unlike methimazole) inhibits peripheral 5'-deiodinase → reduces conversion of T4 to the active T3 — an advantage in severe thyrotoxicosis.\nc) TWO POTENTIAL DRUG-DRUG INTERACTIONS (2 marks):\n1. PTU + warfarin → PTU can potentiate the anticoagulant effect (increased INR/bleeding risk) by reducing vitamin K-dependent clotting factor metabolism.\n2. PTU + other bone-marrow-suppressing drugs (e.g., cytotoxic agents, carbimazole) → additive risk of agranulocytosis.\n3. PTU + amiodarone/iodide-containing agents → reduced antithyroid response (iodine antagonizes).\n(Any two.)"
