              "stem": "Compare and contrast the pharmacokinetics and clinical application of regular insulin and neutral protamine hagedorn insulin. (4 marks)",
              "modelAnswer": "REGULAR INSULIN vs NPH INSULIN (4 marks):\nREGULAR INSULIN (short-acting):\n- Pharmacokinetics: clear soluble crystalline zinc insulin; onset ~30 min SC, peak 2-4 hours, duration 6-8 hours; can be given IV (the only insulin used IV — in DKA/emergencies); given SC 30 minutes before meals.\n- Clinical application: prandial (meal-time) coverage; management of DKA and hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (IV infusion); acute/hospital settings; hyperkalemia (with glucose).\nNPH (NEUTRAL PROTAMINE HAGEDORN) INSULIN (intermediate-acting):\n- Pharmacokinetics: isophane insulin — protamine-complexed, CLOUDY suspension (must be resuspended by rolling); onset 1-2 hours, peak 4-10 hours, duration 18-24 hours; SC use only (never IV); given once or twice daily.\n- Clinical application: BASAL (background) insulin coverage — often combined with regular/rapid insulin in twice-daily or split-mix regimens; used in type 1 and type 2 diabetes.\nCOMPARISON/CONTRAST: regular is short-acting/mealtime, NPH is intermediate-acting/basal; regular is clear and can be given IV, NPH is cloudy and SC-only; NPH has a wider, less predictable peak (hypoglycemia risk), while regular has a more rapid predictable onset; they are often used together (e.g., 30/70 premixed preparations)."
