              "stem": "Describe INSULIN in terms of the\nClasses and examples(4 marks)\nmode of action,(2 marks)\nclinical applications,(3 marks)\nside effects,(3 marks)\ncontraindications(3 marks)",
              "modelAnswer": "INSULIN:\nCLASSES AND EXAMPLES (4 marks):\n1. Rapid-acting analogues: insulin LISPRO, ASPART, GLULISINE (onset ~5-15 min, peak 1-2 h, duration 3-5 h).\n2. Short-acting: REGULAR insulin (onset ~30 min, peak 2-4 h, duration 6-8 h).\n3. Intermediate-acting: NPH (isophane) insulin (onset 1-2 h, peak 4-10 h, duration 18-24 h).\n4. Long-acting: insulin GLARGINE and DETEMIR (peakless, duration ~24 h); ultra-long DEGLUDEC (>42 h).\n5. Premixed: e.g., 70/30 (NPH/regular) or biphasic aspart.\nMODE OF ACTION (2 marks): Insulin binds the insulin receptor (tyrosine kinase) on target cells → autophosphorylation → intracellular signaling → translocation of GLUT4 to the membrane (↑ glucose uptake in muscle/adipose); promotes glycogenesis, lipogenesis and protein synthesis; inhibits gluconeogenesis, glycogenolysis, lipolysis and proteolysis.\nCLINICAL APPLICATIONS (3 marks): Type 1 diabetes mellitus (essential/always required); type 2 diabetes failing oral agents or with severe hyperglycemia, catabolic states, surgery, pregnancy, acute illness; diabetic ketoacidosis and hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (IV regular insulin); gestational diabetes; emergency management of hyperkalemia (with glucose); (intensive therapy to prevent microvascular complications).\nSIDE EFFECTS (3 marks): HYPOGLYCEMIA (most common and most dangerous — sweating, tremor, confusion, coma); weight gain; lipodystrophy/hypertrophy at injection sites; allergic reactions (rare — local or systemic); hypokalemia; insulin edema; (rare) antibody-mediated resistance.\nCONTRAINDICATIONS (3 marks): Absolute — hypoglycemia (current episode); hypersensitivity to insulin or any of its components/excipients. Relative/precautionary — renal impairment and hepatic impairment (reduced insulin requirements — dose reduction and monitoring); (avoid mixing with incompatible preparations)."
