              "stem": "Describe the four phases of clinical studies during drug development (8 marks)",
              "modelAnswer": "FOUR PHASES OF CLINICAL STUDIES IN DRUG DEVELOPMENT (8 marks):\nPHASE 1 (Clinical pharmacology/toxicology): Small number (20-80) of NORMAL HEALTHY VOLUNTEERS (sometimes patients for toxic drugs); conducted by clinical pharmacologists; determines SAFETY, tolerability, pharmacokinetics (ADME), and the MAXIMUM TOLERATED DOSE (MTD).\nPHASE 2 (Therapeutic exploratory): 100-300 PATIENTS with the target disease; assesses EFFICACY, dose-response relationship and short-term safety — often with placebo/standard comparator; establishes the dose for phase 3.\nPHASE 3 (Therapeutic confirmatory): LARGE number of patients (1,000-5,000) with the disease under conditions of proposed use; RANDOMIZED, DOUBLE-BLIND, controlled (vs placebo and/or standard treatment) trials; confirms effectiveness, detects/adverse effects, compares with existing therapy — the basis for registration/marketing approval.\nPHASE 4 (Post-marketing surveillance): After approval — large, long-term monitoring of the drug in the general population; detects RARE or LONG-TERM adverse effects, drug interactions, and new indications; pharmacovigilance (e.g., withdrawal of rofecoxib for cardiovascular events)."
